00:07:24.475 --> 00:07:26.405
- [Voiceover] Andrzej
asks, "How do you keep aim

00:07:26.405 --> 00:07:28.315
"on your goals, and separate yourself from

00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:30.149
"the demands of the external world?"

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:33.638
- Andrzej, I'll take this one first.

00:07:33.638 --> 00:07:36.116
You know, I don't know what to tell you

00:07:36.116 --> 00:07:38.818
other than it's unbelievable for me

00:07:38.818 --> 00:07:41.521
how much the external
world has not factored

00:07:41.521 --> 00:07:43.715
into my decision making,
I've talked about,

00:07:43.715 --> 00:07:45.422
if you've been watching
this show long enough

00:07:45.422 --> 00:07:48.469
that first F on a test in fourth grade

00:07:48.469 --> 00:07:50.318
and literally making that transition

00:07:50.318 --> 00:07:52.106
to I'm gonna fight the market,

00:07:52.106 --> 00:07:54.056
and I've been fighting
the market my whole life.

00:07:54.056 --> 00:07:57.469
I think for me, it was
the level of self esteem

00:07:57.469 --> 00:08:00.029
that my mom instilled
in me, plus some level

00:08:00.029 --> 00:08:03.178
of my own DNA, I think
that's the friction at hand.

00:08:03.178 --> 00:08:06.592
Heck, a lot of the themes of
our last question were on this,

00:08:06.592 --> 00:08:08.095
right, like what does the
market want you to do,

00:08:08.095 --> 00:08:10.432
whether that's your parents or society,

00:08:10.432 --> 00:08:11.833
versus what you want to do.

00:08:11.833 --> 00:08:14.190
For me, it has a lot to do
with intestinal fortitude.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:17.969
A Gorilla Monsoon WWF reference.

00:08:19.099 --> 00:08:22.987
You know, I think it's
surrounding yourself with people

00:08:22.987 --> 00:08:25.974
that give you permission
to take that risk.

00:08:25.974 --> 00:08:28.859
That to me is the most practical version

00:08:28.859 --> 00:08:29.692
of what I'm giving you,

00:08:29.692 --> 00:08:31.378
other than you've got to be born with it.

00:08:31.378 --> 00:08:33.044
It's finding those like minded people

00:08:33.044 --> 00:08:35.807
who are taking those similar risks,

00:08:35.807 --> 00:08:37.900
and give you, through their own actions,

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:41.273
a little more umph, or
if you're amazingly lucky

00:08:41.273 --> 00:08:44.401
to have that parental, or
mentor infrastructure above you

00:08:44.401 --> 00:08:46.291
that created that context.

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:47.185
Case.

00:08:47.185 --> 00:08:50.354
- I think focus is everything.

00:08:50.354 --> 00:08:53.077
I think that you can do 10 things poorly,

00:08:53.077 --> 00:08:55.698
or one thing well, and saying, "No,"

00:08:55.698 --> 00:08:59.538
is something that I only
learned late in my career.

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:00.411
- I still suck at it.

00:09:00.411 --> 00:09:01.488
I still suck at it right now.

00:09:01.488 --> 00:09:04.109
- Saying, "No," is so
hard, but the truth is

00:09:04.109 --> 00:09:05.937
like we're surrounded by leeches,

00:09:05.937 --> 00:09:08.741
blood suckers, and vampires,
and those are people

00:09:08.741 --> 00:09:12.480
that want to take, take, take,
and they don't give back,

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.060
and learning to say,
"No," to those people,

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:17.904
learning to say, "No,"
to all those distractions

00:09:17.904 --> 00:09:19.530
is the only way to get anywhere.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.171
Cause time is finite, life is short.

00:09:22.171 --> 00:09:24.731
Quickly you find things in life
that are really incredible,

00:09:24.731 --> 00:09:26.701
like family, things that you love,

00:09:26.701 --> 00:09:27.758
things that you're passionate about

00:09:27.758 --> 00:09:30.419
that might distract you in a positive way

00:09:30.419 --> 00:09:33.142
from your career focus.

00:09:33.142 --> 00:09:35.174
So, you have to learn
to shed everything else.

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:36.515
- I'm gonna throw a little
bit of a curve ball.

00:09:38.455 --> 00:09:42.606
I get so much happiness out
of doing things for people

00:09:42.606 --> 00:09:44.577
who would be, you know, categorized

00:09:44.577 --> 00:09:47.035
as the way you just broke it down

00:09:47.035 --> 00:09:49.655
because I have a weird gear inside of me

00:09:49.655 --> 00:09:52.540
that has zero expectation for the return

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.775
on someone's selfishness.

00:09:54.775 --> 00:09:56.775
I know that's a little bit of a mouthful,

00:09:56.775 --> 00:09:59.213
but it's just, it's probably why

00:09:59.213 --> 00:10:01.591
I'm so ridiculously happy.

00:10:01.591 --> 00:10:03.927
I have such little
expectation for the return,

00:10:03.927 --> 00:10:07.015
it makes me happy to do the give,

00:10:07.015 --> 00:10:09.412
I sit in front of you
knowing I will accomplish

00:10:09.412 --> 00:10:13.090
less in my career, and amass less wealth,

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:15.599
and a lot of other things,
less time with my family,

00:10:15.599 --> 00:10:19.155
which is my number one because
I get so much happiness

00:10:19.155 --> 00:10:21.247
out of some of those actions.

00:10:21.247 --> 00:10:24.112
So, I would tell you if you're
in a rare group like myself,

00:10:24.112 --> 00:10:26.408
make sure you recognize,
in a world where people

00:10:26.408 --> 00:10:28.082
will tell you that you're
a sucker for doing it,

00:10:28.082 --> 00:10:29.342
or things of that nature, you still got

00:10:29.342 --> 00:10:31.333
to make yourself happy,
but I will tell you,

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:33.690
I'm way happier than I was five years ago

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:37.489
because I have grown in
my no meter moving a ton.

00:10:37.489 --> 00:10:39.602
It hasn't gone to zero,
and I think a lot of people

00:10:39.602 --> 00:10:41.757
close themselves out of serendipity

00:10:41.757 --> 00:10:44.417
by saying no too much, right,
and I think we've probably

00:10:44.417 --> 00:10:47.363
both benefited through
our years of the yes

00:10:47.363 --> 00:10:49.682
when it didn't make sense on paper.

00:10:49.682 --> 00:10:51.914
But I'm with you man,
I mean I made a video

00:10:51.914 --> 00:10:53.865
a long time ago that, The Yes Virus.

00:10:53.865 --> 00:10:56.242
It's like the sickness of just
saying, "Yes," all the time,

00:10:56.242 --> 00:10:57.034
and it's a tough one.

00:10:57.034 --> 00:10:57.908
- Yeah, I mean, I've made movies,

00:10:57.908 --> 00:10:59.696
I made a movie that's Just Say Yes,

00:10:59.696 --> 00:11:01.099
like I believe in saying, "Yes,"

00:11:01.099 --> 00:11:03.759
I believe in embracing
risk, and embracing chance,

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:04.409
and all the things that...

00:11:04.409 --> 00:11:05.324
- Do you think you need to say, "No,"

00:11:05.324 --> 00:11:06.522
more as you get older?

00:11:06.522 --> 00:11:08.087
- I just think it's a learning curve,

00:11:08.087 --> 00:11:09.895
a very steep learning curve to understand

00:11:09.895 --> 00:11:12.374
when no is appropriate and
when yes is appropriate,

00:11:12.374 --> 00:11:15.238
and until you learn
that, you default to yes.

00:11:15.238 --> 00:11:17.514
- It's a really, really, or no,

00:11:17.514 --> 00:11:19.363
my dad defaults to no.

00:11:19.363 --> 00:11:21.435
I think you and I, like
we have some similarities

00:11:21.435 --> 00:11:23.386
that makes, like I think
there's a lot of people there

00:11:23.386 --> 00:11:24.929
that default to no, I think there's

00:11:24.929 --> 00:11:26.433
a lot of people that default.

00:11:26.433 --> 00:11:28.627
My dad's opening words are, "Hey dad."

00:11:28.627 --> 00:11:29.440
"No."

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.569
Like I can't, "I was gonna
say how was your day?"

00:11:32.569 --> 00:11:33.939
You know, like no is not a proper.

00:11:33.939 --> 00:11:36.519
Like, I know a lot of
people that default into no.

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:38.408
I think we happen to be surrounded

00:11:38.408 --> 00:11:40.907
by a lot of people that default into yes.

00:11:40.907 --> 00:11:41.760
- Yeah, lucky us.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.406
- But I think, you know
it'll be interesting,

00:11:43.406 --> 00:11:44.097
you know what actually,

00:11:44.097 --> 00:11:46.249
quick little side question of the day,

00:11:46.249 --> 00:11:49.134
give me are you a
default yes or no person?

00:11:49.134 --> 00:11:52.487
I'm just curious for my
own kind of like polling.

00:11:52.487 --> 00:11:53.772
India, move it along.

00:11:54.335 --> 00:11:56.732
- [Voiceover] CJ asks,
"How has having a family

00:11:56.732 --> 00:11:58.601
"changed your long term view of work?

00:11:58.601 --> 00:12:00.822
"And what does retirement mean to you?"

00:12:01.385 --> 00:12:02.421
- CJ asked a good question.

00:12:02.421 --> 00:12:03.986
I'll let you go, cause I know you're

00:12:03.986 --> 00:12:05.428
a new father of a second.

00:12:05.428 --> 00:12:07.500
- Yeah, so what does family
have to do with work?

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:10.223
I think family is the ultimate cheat,

00:12:10.223 --> 00:12:11.563
and what I mean by that is I had a kid

00:12:11.563 --> 00:12:14.509
when I was 16, I've always had a family

00:12:14.509 --> 00:12:15.668
since I was an adult,

00:12:15.668 --> 00:12:17.902
my entire adult life I've had a family,

00:12:17.902 --> 00:12:21.215
and it gives you a reason
to do all this work,

00:12:21.215 --> 00:12:23.469
and that downtrodden feeling you can have,

00:12:23.469 --> 00:12:24.546
which is like, "Why am I doing this?

00:12:24.546 --> 00:12:26.842
"Why am working another night
til three in the morning?"

00:12:26.842 --> 00:12:30.113
When you have family, for
me, it gives me my purpose,

00:12:30.113 --> 00:12:34.217
my reason for doing
everything I do is my family.

00:12:34.217 --> 00:12:37.956
Part two retirement,
retirement's my biggest fear.

00:12:37.956 --> 00:12:41.428
Retirement is what people
do when they wait to die.

00:12:41.428 --> 00:12:43.664
My grandmother was a tap dancer,

00:12:43.664 --> 00:12:45.015
and she had a tap dance school,

00:12:45.015 --> 00:12:47.411
and she taught tap everyday of her life,

00:12:47.411 --> 00:12:49.423
and she taught tap on a Friday,

00:12:49.423 --> 00:12:51.923
and she died on a Monday when she was 92.

00:12:51.923 --> 00:12:53.466
That's my fantasy.

00:12:53.466 --> 00:12:55.762
I want to work until the last minute,

00:12:55.762 --> 00:12:59.826
I want to be working in my
hospital bed as I'm dying.

00:12:59.826 --> 00:13:01.735
So, that's how I feel about retirement.

00:13:01.735 --> 00:13:03.707
- I'll start with
retirement, I'm you know,

00:13:03.707 --> 00:13:05.493
in the complete same camp.

00:13:05.493 --> 00:13:06.773
You know, that is my nightmare.

00:13:06.773 --> 00:13:10.553
I want to die on Monday, on
the Monday that I'm working.

00:13:10.553 --> 00:13:13.133
I didn't need those two days in between.

00:13:13.133 --> 00:13:16.262
You know, I'm with you
I think, you know look,

00:13:16.262 --> 00:13:17.988
I will say this, there's one weird

00:13:17.988 --> 00:13:19.785
retirement fantasy I have,

00:13:19.785 --> 00:13:21.349
which is to be an old man sitting

00:13:21.349 --> 00:13:23.442
at the racetrack, having some nickname

00:13:23.442 --> 00:13:26.591
like one eyed Gary, and
like betting on the ponies.

00:13:26.591 --> 00:13:29.882
I do like the notion of
the ponies as an old man,

00:13:29.882 --> 00:13:32.320
so there's a little bit of that.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:36.059
You know, the family, work
life balance whole thing

00:13:36.059 --> 00:13:38.334
I think is completely counter punching.

00:13:38.334 --> 00:13:40.833
Meaning, I hate giving an answer to this

00:13:40.833 --> 00:13:44.043
because I think it really is
predicated on your partner,

00:13:44.043 --> 00:13:46.319
and then the evolution of your kids.

00:13:46.319 --> 00:13:48.412
My partner part I really
kind of took care of.

00:13:48.412 --> 00:13:50.431
I mean, I was looking for Lizzie,

00:13:50.431 --> 00:13:52.382
when I found her, I locked her up,

00:13:52.382 --> 00:13:54.190
married her immediately, we were married

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:55.348
within the year of meeting.

00:13:55.348 --> 00:13:58.070
I told her on our first date
that we were getting married.

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.427
I knew that she was independent enough

00:14:00.427 --> 00:14:02.582
and could, I intuitively
felt that she could

00:14:02.582 --> 00:14:05.080
handle the insanity that is me.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.680
It's crazy, I feel like
we're still dating.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:09.509
Because you know I travel so much,

00:14:09.509 --> 00:14:12.597
and like I'm busy, but like
it's just over communication.

00:14:12.597 --> 00:14:15.158
When I see a little
strain, I'll cancel a trip,

00:14:15.158 --> 00:14:17.798
I won't say yes, you know, I try to hack,

00:14:17.798 --> 00:14:19.545
the kids are a whole new variable.

00:14:19.545 --> 00:14:21.741
You know, now that
Misha's five and a half,

00:14:21.741 --> 00:14:23.041
I've got to get ready for:

00:14:23.041 --> 00:14:24.787
They may not be like Lizzie.

00:14:24.787 --> 00:14:26.860
You know, my little Xander might want me

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.790
at every single thing at every moment.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:30.821
So, I'm starting to get mentally prepared

00:14:30.821 --> 00:14:32.773
to counter punch their reality.

00:14:32.773 --> 00:14:34.905
Kids are always going to
want their parents around,

00:14:34.905 --> 00:14:37.201
but what's the hack, right.

00:14:37.201 --> 00:14:39.375
Like, do I, like it's
funny, I've been traveling,

00:14:39.375 --> 00:14:42.728
and where I speak now, I spoke in Anaheim

00:14:42.728 --> 00:14:44.820
and I noticed that Disneyland
was right next door,

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.929
so I'm like, "Maybe I'll do
these speaking engagements

00:14:47.929 --> 00:14:49.880
"cause I'll take the kids,
let them see what dad does,

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:50.855
"and then a full day of..."

00:14:50.855 --> 00:14:52.378
So, it's interesting how my brain

00:14:52.378 --> 00:14:53.422
is starting to adjust to:

00:14:53.422 --> 00:14:55.222
What's their reality gonna be like?

00:14:55.222 --> 00:14:56.421
So, my answer to your question

00:14:56.421 --> 00:14:58.452
is counter punching, what I mean by that

00:14:58.452 --> 00:15:00.627
is gross over communicating.

00:15:00.627 --> 00:15:03.308
Having those conversations
with your spouse

00:15:03.308 --> 00:15:05.585
or with your partner,
having those conversations

00:15:05.585 --> 00:15:08.292
maybe even at an early
age with your children.

00:15:08.875 --> 00:15:10.704
- [Voiceover] Shay asks,
"Think back to a time

00:15:10.704 --> 00:15:12.451
"when you pissed someone off.

00:15:12.451 --> 00:15:14.158
"How badly did you piss them off,

00:15:14.158 --> 00:15:15.708
"and what did you do to make up for it?"

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:17.409
- This is a really interesting question.

00:15:17.409 --> 00:15:18.961
- I work so hard not to piss people off.

00:15:18.961 --> 00:15:19.794
- Oh man, me too.

00:15:19.794 --> 00:15:22.151
I'm like suffocated by
pissing someone off.

00:15:25.251 --> 00:15:27.352
I'll take this, pissing someone off.

00:15:27.352 --> 00:15:30.663
So look, I mean the only
time I'm truly ever affected,

00:15:30.663 --> 00:15:34.965
ever, by pissing someone off
is when I piss myself off.

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:37.986
So, it's a little bit
of a wrinkle, meaning

00:15:37.986 --> 00:15:41.541
I am so aggressive to try
not to piss off anyone,

00:15:41.541 --> 00:15:44.467
that the second I taste
in the water that I did

00:15:44.467 --> 00:15:46.925
I start hedging like a hog.

00:15:46.925 --> 00:15:49.058
You know, like I start like really,

00:15:49.058 --> 00:15:51.375
did you get that Sonic
Hedgehog, good alright,

00:15:51.375 --> 00:15:52.981
you got it (mumbles).

00:15:52.981 --> 00:15:55.336
I'm in full apology mode immediately.

00:15:55.336 --> 00:15:57.368
The only time I even am comfortable

00:15:57.368 --> 00:15:59.177
pissing people off is on stage

00:15:59.177 --> 00:16:00.985
when I'm challenging the audience

00:16:00.985 --> 00:16:03.971
to the quid pro quo, so I'm,

00:16:03.971 --> 00:16:06.206
on a one to one basis, I'm crippled

00:16:06.206 --> 00:16:08.014
by hurting someone's
feelings, and the second

00:16:08.014 --> 00:16:11.041
that I sense it, I start backtracking.

00:16:11.041 --> 00:16:13.907
So, the way I answer, the way I fix it

00:16:13.907 --> 00:16:17.421
is by immediately taking
the gas off the pedal,

00:16:17.421 --> 00:16:19.534
cause I don't like that feeling.

00:16:19.534 --> 00:16:21.952
I think there's other ways
to manipulate my point.

00:16:21.952 --> 00:16:23.902
That's the truth, I truly think that I can

00:16:23.902 --> 00:16:27.467
drive home my point
through honey, not vinegar.

00:16:27.467 --> 00:16:29.823
- Yeah, I agree, I mean
there's no worse feeling

00:16:29.823 --> 00:16:32.449
than upsetting someone else.

00:16:33.743 --> 00:16:35.612
And that said, because I
have a very specific image

00:16:35.612 --> 00:16:37.908
in my mind when I think
of pissing someone off.

00:16:37.908 --> 00:16:40.061
But I can tell you that living life

00:16:40.061 --> 00:16:41.931
where you pander to other people

00:16:41.931 --> 00:16:43.474
to make sure you never have enemies

00:16:43.474 --> 00:16:45.140
or never have anyone dislike you

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.193
is a very dangerous game to play,

00:16:47.193 --> 00:16:48.555
and I've always said with my work

00:16:48.555 --> 00:16:50.951
that I'd rather, if 100 people see

00:16:50.951 --> 00:16:52.312
a movie I make, I would rather have

00:16:52.312 --> 00:16:54.933
50 people love and 50 people hate it

00:16:54.933 --> 00:16:57.474
than have 100 people go, "Eh."

00:16:57.474 --> 00:17:02.370
So, it's a fine line in that
idea of pissing someone off

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:04.218
can mean so many different things.

00:17:04.218 --> 00:17:06.372
The last time somebody
walked away from me angry

00:17:06.372 --> 00:17:08.911
I honestly can't remember,
but the last time

00:17:08.911 --> 00:17:11.694
I read the YouTube comments
on any one of my videos

00:17:11.694 --> 00:17:14.864
and they were 100% positive,

00:17:14.864 --> 00:17:16.367
well there was never that last time,

00:17:16.367 --> 00:17:17.546
cause I don't think that's ever happened.

00:17:17.546 --> 00:17:20.756
- Yeah, I mean, that just
made me think of Linkedin.

00:17:20.756 --> 00:17:23.133
Boy, does the conservative
business world hate

00:17:23.133 --> 00:17:24.049
where I'm coming from.

00:17:24.049 --> 00:17:25.368
You know, it's funny,
I'm sure a lot of people

00:17:25.368 --> 00:17:26.365
are watching right now and saying,

00:17:26.365 --> 00:17:27.095
"What?"

00:17:27.095 --> 00:17:29.289
My living is being in the minority

00:17:29.289 --> 00:17:31.341
on my points of view, on the current state

00:17:31.341 --> 00:17:33.597
of marketing and all that stuff,

00:17:33.597 --> 00:17:36.949
but I'm with you, like on
a one to one basis, never.

00:17:36.949 --> 00:17:39.793
As little as possible, to
the masses, no problem.

00:17:39.793 --> 00:17:41.276
And I think that's an interesting insight.

00:17:41.276 --> 00:17:44.365
- Yeah, I think so, cause as a human being

00:17:44.365 --> 00:17:45.848
the ambition is to never
piss someone else off,

00:17:45.848 --> 00:17:47.656
or to leave someone else upset,

00:17:47.656 --> 00:17:49.753
but as far as a belief system.
- For sure.

00:17:49.753 --> 00:17:51.313
- The belief system has
to be uniquely yours,

00:17:51.313 --> 00:17:52.369
even if that's a divisive...

00:17:52.369 --> 00:17:56.575
- I will also say I'm in the sport sense,

00:17:56.575 --> 00:17:59.582
like in competition, I'm very interested

00:17:59.582 --> 00:18:01.024
in pissing people off.

00:18:01.024 --> 00:18:03.951
I'm, you know, it matters to me a lot,

00:18:03.951 --> 00:18:06.511
like nothing excites
me more than if I know

00:18:06.511 --> 00:18:09.395
somebody's upset because
I did something positive

00:18:09.395 --> 00:18:10.920
for my team versus theirs.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:14.515
It's extremely interesting
to me to piss people off

00:18:14.515 --> 00:18:16.487
during the heat of battle
because I want to get

00:18:16.487 --> 00:18:18.274
them emotional and off their game.

00:18:19.274 --> 00:18:20.102
- Yeah.

00:18:20.102 --> 00:18:21.266
- Yeah, alright, let's move on.

00:18:22.053 --> 00:18:23.658
- [Voiceover] Hamms
asks, "If you could have

00:18:23.658 --> 00:18:26.847
"a bionic body part, which
body part would it be,

00:18:26.847 --> 00:18:28.316
"and what powers would it have?"

00:18:29.143 --> 00:18:32.394
- The truth is I do
have a bionic body part.

00:18:32.394 --> 00:18:34.446
My right leg from the knee to the hip,

00:18:34.446 --> 00:18:36.072
and then the hip all the way in

00:18:36.072 --> 00:18:37.636
is made out of titanium.

00:18:37.636 --> 00:18:38.326
- I didn't know that.

00:18:38.326 --> 00:18:40.460
- Yeah, most people don't.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.568
And the super power that it gives me

00:18:43.597 --> 00:18:47.660
I think I can make this, but
when I was in the hospital,

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.444
my leg broken in like,
literally, 27 places,

00:18:50.444 --> 00:18:51.607
and they were hammering
it back together

00:18:51.607 --> 00:18:54.556
made out of metal, the doctor said to me,

00:18:55.586 --> 00:18:57.131
"You know, you'll never run again.

00:18:57.131 --> 00:18:58.714
"You'll be able to like
catch up with a taxi cab,

00:18:58.714 --> 00:19:00.398
"but you will never run again."

00:19:00.398 --> 00:19:02.855
And I was 26 years old,
and that is a tough thing

00:19:02.855 --> 00:19:04.765
for a 26 year old to
hear, especially someone

00:19:04.765 --> 00:19:06.817
as physical as I am.

00:19:06.817 --> 00:19:08.442
So, the superpower that it gave me

00:19:08.442 --> 00:19:10.494
was prior to that,

00:19:10.494 --> 00:19:14.193
prior to getting my bionic limb,

00:19:14.193 --> 00:19:16.488
I wasn't much of an athlete or a runner,

00:19:16.488 --> 00:19:17.971
but since getting my metal leg,

00:19:17.971 --> 00:19:20.978
I've run 22 marathons, and
four IRONMAN Triathlons,

00:19:20.978 --> 00:19:23.769
and countless other races,
and I'm a much fitter,

00:19:23.769 --> 00:19:26.187
faster person since getting my bionic leg.

00:19:26.187 --> 00:19:28.605
So it has, I do have a bionic limb,

00:19:28.605 --> 00:19:30.739
and I do have a superpower.

00:19:30.739 --> 00:19:33.075
- I also have a bionic limb that no,

00:19:33.075 --> 00:19:35.696
no I don't, I really wanted
one though, I'm pissed.

00:19:35.696 --> 00:19:36.591
- I was excited to hear what limb,

00:19:36.591 --> 00:19:37.646
what you were gonna go with.

00:19:37.646 --> 00:19:39.739
- You know what, I would go with ears.

00:19:39.739 --> 00:19:43.599
I'm not joking, I'm very intrigued

00:19:43.599 --> 00:19:47.628
by the notion of like, I
love picking up senses right,

00:19:47.628 --> 00:19:49.948
and for some reason, so one of the things

00:19:49.948 --> 00:19:53.258
that I think I do extremely well is I,

00:19:53.258 --> 00:19:55.554
that I really enjoy being able to do,

00:19:55.554 --> 00:19:56.854
and I'd love to do more of, which is why

00:19:56.854 --> 00:19:59.190
I'm going with ears, is I am often

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:01.100
at a dinner table, or at a conference,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:02.908
having a conversation fully in it,

00:20:02.908 --> 00:20:05.550
and capable of listening
to the two or three

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.703
other side conversations,
as a matter of fact,

00:20:07.703 --> 00:20:09.349
often when I talk to a small group

00:20:09.349 --> 00:20:13.006
of 40 or 50 people, I
will use side whispering

00:20:13.006 --> 00:20:16.135
of what I just said to
incorporate into my talk,

00:20:16.135 --> 00:20:18.207
and then look at the person,
and they're always freaked out,

00:20:18.207 --> 00:20:19.792
because I was so focusing going here,

00:20:19.792 --> 00:20:21.864
but I heard them, and so I think people

00:20:21.864 --> 00:20:24.505
don't listen enough. That's
a big thesis of mine.

00:20:24.505 --> 00:20:27.432
It's my overall social media thesis,

00:20:27.432 --> 00:20:29.253
and so I'm gonna go with ears.

00:20:30.365 --> 00:20:33.129
- I'm just picturing Gary
with gigantic, titanium

00:20:33.129 --> 00:20:34.855
- It's like metal ears.

00:20:34.855 --> 00:20:36.277
- ears that turn and respond to people.

00:20:36.277 --> 00:20:37.882
- Case, one of the things
we do on this show,

00:20:37.882 --> 00:20:40.137
I didn't tell you, but I'm sure
you're busy doing your thing

00:20:40.137 --> 00:20:41.844
is we get to ask a question of the day

00:20:41.844 --> 00:20:43.958
of the VaynerNation, I
know you're not prepped,

00:20:43.958 --> 00:20:45.582
and so that makes it even more fun for me,

00:20:45.582 --> 00:20:46.903
but I'm just trying to
talk a little longer

00:20:46.903 --> 00:20:47.845
to give you a couple seconds.

00:20:47.845 --> 00:20:48.191
- I appreciate it.

00:20:48.191 --> 00:20:50.386
- You get to ask them
any question you like.

00:20:50.386 --> 00:20:51.706
We're gonna end this show with you asking

00:20:51.706 --> 00:20:54.083
the VaynerNation, VaynerNation please,

00:20:54.083 --> 00:20:55.505
our first ever guest, so get in.

00:20:55.505 --> 00:20:57.679
Lurkers, this is a good time to come out.

00:20:57.679 --> 00:20:59.284
Please ask your question of the day.

